202 — Pushing to Container Registries
Publish Docker images to registries automatically. Master Docker Hub, GitHub Container Registry (GHCR), authentication, and automated push workflows for deployable images.

Step 1 Create Docker Hub access token
Generate a secure token for CI/CD authentication.

Commands to Run
echo 'Visit: https://hub.docker.com/settings/security'
echo 'Click: New Access Token'
echo 'Description: GitHub Actions'
echo 'Permissions: Read, Write, Delete'
echo 'Copy token - you will not see it again!'
What This Does
Access tokens are more secure than passwords. They can be revoked independently and have granular permissions. Never commit tokens to Git.

Expected Outcome
Token generated and copied to clipboard. Keep it safe - you'll add it to GitHub secrets next.

Pro Tips
1. Use access tokens, never passwords in CI
2. Tokens can be revoked without changing password
3. Set minimal permissions needed
4. Store in password manager immediately
5. Never commit tokens to Git

Step 2 Add credentials to GitHub secrets
Store Docker Hub credentials securely in repository.

Commands to Run
cd docker-ci-app
gh secret set DOCKER_USERNAME --body "your-dockerhub-username"
gh secret set DOCKER_TOKEN --body "paste-token-here"
gh secret list

What This Does
GitHub Secrets encrypt and store sensitive data. Accessible in workflows as ${{ secrets.NAME }}. Never exposed in logs. This is how to handle credentials in CI/CD.

Expected Outcome
Two secrets created: DOCKER_USERNAME and DOCKER_TOKEN. Listed but values hidden (encrypted).

Pro Tips
1. Secrets are encrypted at rest and in transit
2. Not visible in workflow logs (auto-masked)
3. Use gh secret set for command-line management
4. Alternative: add via GitHub UI → Settings → Secrets
5. Secrets available to all workflows in repo

Step 3 Create Docker Hub push workflow
Build and push images to Docker Hub automatically.

Commands to Run
cat > .github/workflows/dockerhub-push.yml << 'EOF'
name: Push to Docker Hub

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main ]
    tags:
      - 'v*.*.*'

jobs:
  build-push:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    
    steps:
    - name: Checkout
      uses: actions/checkout@v4
    
    - name: Set up Docker Buildx
      uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3
    
    - name: Login to Docker Hub
      uses: docker/login-action@v3
      with:
        username: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}
        password: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_TOKEN }}
    
    - name: Extract metadata
      id: meta
      uses: docker/metadata-action@v5
      with:
        images: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}/docker-ci-app
        tags: |
          type=ref,event=branch
          type=semver,pattern={{version}}
          type=semver,pattern={{major}}.{{minor}}
          type=sha
    
    - name: Build and push
      uses: docker/build-push-action@v5
      with:
        context: .
        push: true
        tags: ${{ steps.meta.outputs.tags }}
        labels: ${{ steps.meta.outputs.labels }}
EOF

git add .github/workflows/dockerhub-push.yml
git commit -m 'Add Docker Hub push workflow'
git push
gh run watch

What This Does
login-action authenticates with Docker Hub using secrets. push: true actually pushes to registry (vs. local-only build). metadata-action generates smart tags based on branch/tag.

Expected Outcome
Workflow authenticates, builds, and pushes to Docker Hub. Image visible at hub.docker.com/r/[username]/docker-ci-app with multiple tags.

Pro Tips
1. push: true is what actually uploads to registry
2. Login must happen before push
3. Secrets never appear in logs (masked automatically)
4. Multiple tags pushed simultaneously (no extra cost)
5. First push may take 2-3 minutes

Step 4 Verify image on Docker Hub
Check published image and pull it locally.

Commands to Run
docker build -t %DOCKER_USERNAME%/docker-ci-app:main . docker push %DOCKER_USERNAME%/docker-ci-app:main
echo 'Visit: https://hub.docker.com/r/'"$DOCKER_USERNAME"'/docker-ci-app'
docker pull $DOCKER_USERNAME/docker-ci-app:main
docker run -d -p 3000:3000 --name registry-test $DOCKER_USERNAME/docker-ci-app:main
curl http://localhost:3000
docker stop registry-test && docker rm registry-test

What This Does
Docker Hub is public by default. Anyone can docker pull your-username/image:tag. Verify image works by pulling and running from registry, not local build.

Expected Outcome
Image pulls from Docker Hub (not local cache). Container runs successfully. Curl returns JSON response. Image is publicly accessible.

Pro Tips
1. Replace $DOCKER_USERNAME with your actual username
2. Public images are free on Docker Hub
3. Private repos: 1 free, then paid
4. docker pull downloads from registry
5. Test pulling on different machine to verify

Step 5 Set up GitHub Container Registry
Use GHCR for tight GitHub integration and unlimited public images.

Commands to Run
gh auth refresh -s write:packages
Copy
cat > .github/workflows/ghcr-push.yml << 'EOF'
name: Push to GHCR

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main ]
    tags:
      - 'v*.*.*'

jobs:
  build-push:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
      packages: write
    
    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4
    
    - name: Set up Docker Buildx
      uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3
    
    - name: Login to GitHub Container Registry
      uses: docker/login-action@v3
      with:
        registry: ghcr.io
        username: ${{ github.actor }}
        password: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
    
    - name: Extract metadata
      id: meta
      uses: docker/metadata-action@v5
      with:
        images: ghcr.io/${{ github.repository_owner }}/docker-ci-app
        tags: |
          type=ref,event=branch
          type=semver,pattern={{version}}
          type=sha
    
    - name: Build and push
      uses: docker/build-push-action@v5
      with:
        context: .
        push: true
        tags: ${{ steps.meta.outputs.tags }}
        labels: ${{ steps.meta.outputs.labels }}
EOF

git add .github/workflows/ghcr-push.yml
git commit -m 'Add GHCR push workflow'
git push
What This Does
GHCR (ghcr.io) is GitHub's container registry. Uses built-in GITHUB_TOKEN (no extra secrets needed). permissions: packages: write grants access. Integrated with GitHub UI.

Expected Outcome
Image pushed to ghcr.io/[username]/docker-ci-app. Visible in GitHub → Packages tab. No extra authentication setup needed.

Pro Tips
1. GHCR: unlimited public packages for free
2. GITHUB_TOKEN automatic - no manual secrets
3. permissions: packages: write required
4. Images linked to GitHub repo automatically
5. Better for GitHub-centric workflows

Step 6 Pull from GitHub Container Registry
Authenticate and pull images from GHCR.

Commands to Run
echo $GITHUB_TOKEN | docker login ghcr.io -u $GITHUB_USER --password-stdin
docker build -t ghcr.io/deepshikha-dubey/docker-ci-app:main .
docker push ghcr.io/deepshikha-dubey/docker-ci-app:main
docker pull ghcr.io/$GITHUB_USER/docker-ci-app:main
docker run -d -p 3000:3000 --name ghcr-test ghcr.io/$GITHUB_USER/docker-ci-app:main
curl http://localhost:3000
docker stop ghcr-test && docker rm ghcr-test
docker logout ghcr.io

What This Does
Public GHCR images don't require auth to pull, but best practice to authenticate. Uses GitHub PAT (Personal Access Token) or gh CLI for credentials.

Expected Outcome
Image pulls from GHCR successfully. Container runs. Always logout after to avoid credential leaks.

Pro Tips
1. Replace $GITHUB_USER with your GitHub username
2. Public packages: no auth needed to pull
3. Private packages: requires authentication
4. Use gh auth token for GITHUB_TOKEN value
5. Always docker logout on shared machines

Step 7 Implement multi-registry push
Push same image to multiple registries simultaneously.

Commands to Run
cat > .github/workflows/multi-registry.yml << 'EOF'
name: Multi-Registry Push

on:
  push:
    tags:
      - 'v*.*.*'

jobs:
  build-push:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
      packages: write
    
    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4
    
    - name: Set up Docker Buildx
      uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3
    
    - name: Login to Docker Hub
      uses: docker/login-action@v3
      with:
        username: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}
        password: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_TOKEN }}
    
    - name: Login to GHCR
      uses: docker/login-action@v3
      with:
        registry: ghcr.io
        username: ${{ github.actor }}
        password: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
    
    - name: Extract metadata
      id: meta
      uses: docker/metadata-action@v5
      with:
        images: |
          ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}/docker-ci-app
          ghcr.io/${{ github.repository_owner }}/docker-ci-app
        tags: |
          type=semver,pattern={{version}}
          type=semver,pattern={{major}}.{{minor}}
    
    - name: Build and push to all registries
      uses: docker/build-push-action@v5
      with:
        context: .
        push: true
        tags: ${{ steps.meta.outputs.tags }}
        labels: ${{ steps.meta.outputs.labels }}
EOF

cat .github/workflows/multi-registry.yml

What This Does
Multiple login-action calls authenticate to different registries. metadata-action with multiple images: creates tags for all. One build, multiple pushes - efficient.

Expected Outcome
Workflow pushes same image to Docker Hub AND GHCR simultaneously. Both registries contain identical images with same tags.

Pro Tips
1. Login to all registries before pushing
2. List all target images in metadata-action
3. Image built once, pushed multiple times (efficient)
4. Use for redundancy or different audiences
5. Triggers only on version tags (v*.*.*)


