Step 9 Implement retention policies
Clean up old images automatically to save storage.

Commands to Run
cat > .github/workflows/cleanup.yml << 'EOF'
name: Cleanup Old Images

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: '0 0 * * 0'  # Weekly on Sunday
  workflow_dispatch:  # Manual trigger

jobs:
  cleanup:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      packages: write
    
    steps:
    - name: Delete old GHCR images
      uses: actions/delete-package-versions@v5
      with:
        package-name: 'docker-ci-app'
        package-type: 'container'
        min-versions-to-keep: 10
        delete-only-untagged-versions: 'true'
EOF

cat .github/workflows/cleanup.yml

What This Does
Scheduled cleanup prevents GHCR storage bloat. Keeps 10 recent versions, deletes untagged (usually intermediates). Runs weekly. workflow_dispatch allows manual triggering.

Expected Outcome
Workflow scheduled to run weekly. Keeps recent images, removes old untagged versions. Prevents hitting storage limits.

Pro Tips
1. schedule: cron runs periodically
2. min-versions-to-keep preserves recent images
3. delete-only-untagged-versions: true keeps releases
4. workflow_dispatch allows manual cleanup
5. GHCR has storage limits even on free tier

Step 10 Add image provenance attestation
Sign images with provenance metadata for security and compliance.

Commands to Run
cat > .github/workflows/provenance.yml << 'EOF'
name: Build with Provenance

on:
  push:
    tags:
      - 'v*.*.*'

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
      packages: write
      id-token: write  # For provenance
      attestations: write
    
    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4
    
    - name: Set up Docker Buildx
      uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3
    
    - name: Login to GHCR
      uses: docker/login-action@v3
      with:
        registry: ghcr.io
        username: ${{ github.actor }}
        password: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
    
    - name: Extract metadata
      id: meta
      uses: docker/metadata-action@v5
      with:
        images: ghcr.io/${{ github.repository_owner }}/docker-ci-app
    
    - name: Build and push with provenance
      uses: docker/build-push-action@v5
      with:
        context: .
        push: true
        tags: ${{ steps.meta.outputs.tags }}
        provenance: true
        sbom: true
EOF

cat .github/workflows/provenance.yml

What This Does
provenance: true creates attestation showing: who built, when, from what source. sbom: true (Software Bill of Materials) lists all dependencies. Critical for supply chain security.

Expected Outcome
Images include provenance and SBOM attestations. Users can verify image authenticity. Compliance with security standards.

Pro Tips
1. Provenance proves image origin
2. SBOM lists all software components
3. Required for many enterprise environments
4. id-token: write needed for signing
5. View attestations with: docker buildx imagetools inspect

Step 11 Test full publish workflow
Create release tag to trigger complete publish pipeline.

Commands to Run
git tag -a v1.0.0 -m 'Release version 1.0.0'
git push origin v1.0.0
gh run watch
docker pull $DOCKER_USERNAME/docker-ci-app:1.0.0
docker pull ghcr.io/$GITHUB_USER/docker-ci-app:1.0.0
docker images | grep docker-ci-app

What This Does
Version tag triggers all registry push workflows. Creates v1.0.0, 1.0, and latest tags on both Docker Hub and GHCR. Full production release process.

Expected Outcome
Workflows trigger on tag. Images pushed to both registries with semantic version tags. Successfully pulled from both sources.

Pro Tips
1. Semantic versioning: v{major}.{minor}.{patch}
2. Tag triggers release workflows
3. Creates: exact (1.0.0), minor (1.0), and latest
4. Both registries get same versions
5. Users can pin to exact version or track latest


